t.card(sl('02','DIAGRAM'),'02',basic+' Explain equilibrium using a diagram constructed from these schedules. Include the quantity imbalance at N$6 and the direction of price pressure if competitive adjustment is possible.','Plot price vertically and quantity horizontally; label D and S. The original equilibrium is N$8 and 80 rolls. At N$6, Qd is 100 and Qs is 60, so excess demand is 40. The shortage can put upward pressure on price towards equilibrium.',['Axes/labels (1): N$ per roll and rolls per week. Original curves (2): correct D (1), correct S (1). Original equilibrium (1): N$8/80. Change/control (1): compare at N$6, not a curve shift. Explanation/interpretation (2): excess demand of 40 (1), upward price pressure under competitive adjustment (1). Total 7.'])
t.card(sl('02','INTERP'),'02',basic+' Analyse the imbalance at N$10 and the possible adjustment towards equilibrium.','At N$10 supply is 100 rolls and demand 60, so excess supply is 40. Unsold offers can encourage sellers to reduce price; a lower price raises quantity demanded and reduces quantity supplied along the existing curves towards their intersection.',['Relationship/pattern (1): excess supply. Evidence/comparison (1): 100 offered versus 60 demanded, gap 40. Explanation/reason (2): unsold offers create downward pressure (1), movements along existing curves can close the gap (1). Total 4.'])
t.card(sl('03','DIAGRAM'),'03',basic+' '+dchange+' Explain the change using a diagram of the original and new equilibria.','Plot D and S from original data, then D1 to the right using the new demand schedule. D/S meet at N$8/80; D1/S meet at N$10/100. The demand increase creates excess demand at the original price, so the new equilibrium moves along unchanged supply to a higher price and quantity.',['Axes/labels (1): correct units. Original curves (2): correct D (1), S (1). Original equilibrium (1): N$8/80. Change (1): D1 right with S fixed. New equilibrium (1): N$10/100. Explanation/interpretation (1): increased demand raises price/quantity through movement along supply. Total 7.'])
t.card(sl('03','INTERP'),'03',basic+' '+schang+' Analyse the equilibrium change and distinguish the changed relationship from movement along a curve.','Supply increases at each price, shifting S right. The original equilibrium N$8/80 becomes N$6/100. The fall in price increases quantity demanded along unchanged D; demand has not shifted.',['Relationship/pattern (1): supply shifts right. Evidence/comparison (1): N$8/80 to N$6/100. Explanation/reason (2): production conditions offer more at each price (1), lower price produces movement along unchanged demand (1). Total 4.'])
t.card(sl('03','MISCON'),'03',basic+' Explain the error in shifting demand left merely to show an imposed maximum price of N$6. No demand or supply determinant changes.','The control selects a price on the existing curves; it does not alter the underlying schedules. At N$6 demand is 100 and supply 60, so the control creates a shortage of 40 rather than a demand shift.',['Confusion/boundary (1): imposed price differs from determinant/curve shift. Test to distinguish (1): compare quantities on unchanged schedules at the controlled price.'])
t.card(sl('04','FEATURE'),'04','Identify one non-price determinant of bread demand and one determinant of bread supply.','Buyers’ income for demand; flour input cost for supply.',['Feature/example (1): buyers’ income. Distinction (1): flour cost is a producer/supply condition.'])
t.card(sl('04','MISCON','MIS-6145-008'),'04','Explain the distinction between a rise in buyers’ income and a rise in flour costs as causes of changes in the bread market. Assume bread is a normal good.','Higher income can increase buyers’ demand at each price; higher flour costs can reduce producers’ supply at each price. One changes buyers’ conditions and the other production costs.',['Confusion/difference (1): buyer condition versus producer condition. Test to distinguish (1): identify which side offers/buys a different quantity at the same price.'])
t.card(sl('04','MISCON','MIS-6145-026'),'04','Explain the difference between an income rise shifting demand for a normal good and the availability of substitutes affecting its price responsiveness.','The income rise changes quantity demanded at each price and shifts demand. Substitute availability helps explain how strongly quantity demanded responds to the good’s own price; it is not the same question as a specified income-driven shift.',['Confusion/boundary (1): position/demand shift versus responsiveness. Test to distinguish (1): compare non-price change at each price with response to an own-price change.'])
t.card(sl('04-M01','MECH'),'04-M01','Explain how higher income can increase demand for bread rolls when they are a normal good and other demand conditions are unchanged.','Higher income gives buyers more purchasing power. For the stated normal good, they are willing and able to buy more at each price, shifting demand right and potentially raising equilibrium price and quantity if supply is unchanged and upward sloping.',['Starting change (1): higher income. Because/mechanism (1): greater purchasing power for a normal good. Relationship (1): more demanded at each price/right shift. Consequence (1): higher equilibrium price/quantity with stated supply conditions. Total 4.'])
t.card(sl('04-M01','APP'),'04-M01','Explain the demand change for bread if a stated substitute becomes more expensive, with bread’s own price and other conditions unchanged.','Some buyers switch towards bread, so more bread is demanded at each price and its demand curve shifts right.',['Context (1): substitute dearer at unchanged bread price. Reason/conclusion (1): switching increases bread demand at each price.'])
t.card(sl('04-M02','MECH'),'04-M02','Explain how cheaper flour can increase bread supply, other production and demand conditions unchanged.','Lower input cost reduces the cost of making bread. More output can be worthwhile at the same selling prices, shifting supply right. With downward-sloping demand unchanged, the new equilibrium has a lower price and greater quantity.',['Starting change (1): cheaper flour. Because/mechanism (1): lower production cost. Relationship (1): more supplied at each price/right shift. Consequence (1): lower equilibrium price and higher quantity with stated demand. Total 4.'])
t.card(sl('04-M02','APP'),'04-M02','Explain the supply effect of an increase in flour cost for an invented bread seller, with the bread price and other conditions unchanged.','The higher input cost makes production less attractive at that price, so less is offered at each price and supply contracts left.',['Context (1): flour cost rises. Reason/conclusion (1): lower quantity offered at each price contracts supply.'])
t.card(sl('05','DIAGRAM'),'05',basic+' '+scontract+' Explain the price and quantity change using a constructed diagram.','Plot original D/S at N$8/80. Plot S2 left using the new supply schedule. Its intersection with D is N$10/60. Higher production cost contracts supply, raising the equilibrium price but reducing the quantity traded along unchanged demand.',['Axes/labels (1): N$ per roll and rolls per week. Original curves (2): correct D (1), S (1). Original equilibrium (1): N$8/80. Change (1): S2 left due to input cost. New equilibrium (1): N$10/60. Explanation/interpretation (1): price rises and quantity falls with D unchanged. Total 7.'])
t.card(sl('05','INTERP'),'05',basic+' '+scontract+' Analyse the implications of the equilibrium change for buyers and sellers. Do not infer profit from price alone.','Equilibrium changes from N$8/80 to N$10/60. Buyers face a higher price and fewer rolls. Sellers receive a higher price per roll but trade fewer, while input costs have risen, so a higher price alone cannot establish a gain in receipts or profit.',['Relationship/pattern (1): higher price, lower traded quantity. Evidence/comparison (1): N$8/80 versus N$10/60. Explanation/reason (2): buyers’ affordability/availability worsens (1), seller gains cannot be inferred without quantity/cost effects (1). Total 4.'])
