t.card(p,'01 03 02 04',basic+' '+dchange+' Describe three features of the original demand/supply schedules (6). Explain the new equilibrium and the adjustment from the original price (7). Discuss effects on buyers and sellers, contrasting the demand increase with a supply contraction caused by higher input costs. For the contraction, supplied quantities are 0, 20, 40, 60 and 80 at the stated prices and original demand applies (7).','The original schedule specifies willingness/ability at alternative prices and a common weekly product quantity; demand slopes down and supply up. Increased demand shifts D right; at N$8 the new demand of 120 exceeds supply of 80, creating upward pressure. The new equilibrium is N$10 and 100 rolls. A supply contraction instead meets original demand at N$10 and 60. Both raise price but quantity differs. Demand growth can expand selling opportunities while buyers face a higher price; cost-driven contraction reduces availability and burdens producers with higher costs. Judge gains by the cause, affected agent and costs rather than the price sign alone.',['Context/data description (6): alternative prices (1), N$ per roll stated (1); common quantities/period (1), rolls per week (1); demand falls as own price rises (1), supply rises along original schedule (1). Mechanism/explanation (7): D shifts right (1), at N$8 new Qd120 (1), Qs80 (1), excess demand40 (1), upward price pressure (1), new price N$10 (1), quantity100 (1). Both sides/judgement (7): demand case price/quantity both higher (1), supply case price higher but quantity60 (1); buyers face higher price (1), demand growth expands seller opportunities (1); contraction brings higher costs/lower availability (1); no universal winner from price alone (1), conclude using agent/cause/cost conditions (1). Marks allocation 6+7+7=20.'])
p=t.wo['performance_tasks'][2]
t.card(p,'02 04','Evaluate the consequences of two changes in an invented bread market in separate seven-mark plans. Original equilibrium is N$8 and 80 rolls per week. Case 1: preferences shift demand right with supply unchanged, producing N$10 and 100 rolls. Case 2: higher flour cost contracts supply with demand unchanged, producing N$10 and 60 rolls. Consider buyers, sellers, the cause of the change and the limits of judging benefit from price alone.','Case 1: more wanted output can be traded, benefiting sellers’ opportunities and buyers who obtain additional output. Existing buyers face a higher price. Judge seller benefit subject to costs and the ability to supply, while recognising the distributional effect on buyers. Case 2: sellers receive more per roll but sell fewer and face higher input costs; buyers face less availability and a higher price. This is less favourable for buyers and cannot be assumed beneficial to producers. The decisive conditions are quantity response and costs, rather than the shared price increase.',['Case 1 allocation (7): argument for more wanted output traded (1), expanded seller opportunity (1); argument against higher buyer price (1), producers still need capacity/resources (1); condition costs/capacity relative to demand gain (1); judgement conditional seller gain (1), acknowledge buyer affordability trade-off (1). Case 2 marks (7): argument for higher receipts per roll (1), possible partial recovery of cost pressure (1); argument against fewer rolls traded (1), higher input costs and buyer price burden (1); condition quantity and cost effects (1); judgement rejects automatic seller gain (1), identifies adverse buyer availability/affordability (1). Total 14.'])
# Independent numeric controls on the authored schedules, not a rendered picture.
